# ─── Python / ml-service (S.3) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# ml-service/.env is already covered by `**/*.env` above; .env.example is not,
# and is committed on purpose as the configuration contract.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
ml-service/.venv/
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# Model artifacts. `<key>_latest.joblib` IS COMMITTED — a fresh clone has to be
# able to serve a real model without first running a training pipeline, which is
# what makes the demo reliable and lets a supervisor check out the repo and see the
# feature work. Timestamped runs are the provenance trail on the training machine
# and are reproducible from train_pricing.py plus its recorded seed, so keeping
# every one of them in git would bloat the repo for no benefit.
ml-service/models/*.joblib
!ml-service/models/*_latest.joblib

# Generated training data. The GENERATOR plus its seed is the reproducibility
# story, not the CSV — `python training/generate_bookings.py --seed 42` rebuilds it
# byte for byte. bookings_meta.json is small and stays in git, so the exact
# parameters behind a model are versioned even though the rows are not.
ml-service/data/*.csv
ml-service/data/*.parquet

# ─── Sentiment corpus (S.4) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# The rule above only covers data/ itself, not data/sentiment/, which holds ~9 MB
# of third-party downloads (RUSA, TweetEval) next to the small files that ARE the
# project's own work. So this is default-deny with explicit exceptions: a raw
# dataset added later is ignored until someone deliberately un-ignores it, which is
# the right default when the accident being prevented is redistributing someone
# else's licensed corpus.
#
# `sentiment/*` and NOT `sentiment/` on purpose — ignoring a DIRECTORY stops git
# descending into it at all, and every `!` exception below would then be dead.
ml-service/data/sentiment/*

# Committed, and why each one has to be:
#   README.md            where every source came from, and under what licence
#   authored*.csv        hand-written in-domain rows — our own work, unreproducible
#   .backup_authored*    pre-strip copy, so "85 template clones removed" is auditable
#   domain_test_200.csv  THE REL-8 EXAM. Never regenerate, never train on it.
#   domain_test_meta.json / train.meta.json
#                        sha256s + provenance. These are what let a fresh clone
#                        VERIFY the corpus it rebuilds instead of trusting it, which
#                        is what makes shipping the metadata (but not the 9 MB of
#                        raw text) a real reproducibility story rather than a dodge.
!ml-service/data/sentiment/README.md
!ml-service/data/sentiment/authored*.csv
!ml-service/data/sentiment/.backup_authored*.csv
!ml-service/data/sentiment/domain_test_200.csv
!ml-service/data/sentiment/domain_test_meta.json
!ml-service/data/sentiment/train.meta.json

# ml-service/reports/ is deliberately NOT ignored. metrics.json, the calibration
# plots and model_card.md are the AI evidence trail this milestone exists to
# produce — they are the only inspectable record of what the model claims, and they
# belong in version control next to the code that generated them.

# ─── Assistant intent corpus (S.6) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Same default-deny shape as the sentiment block above, and `assistant/*` not
# `assistant/` for the same reason. What is ignored here is the GENERATED corpus:
# `python training/gen_intents.py` rebuilds intents.csv byte for byte from the
# three hand-written inputs plus its recorded seed, so the generator is the
# reproducibility story and the 200 KB of expanded rows is not.
ml-service/data/assistant/*

# Committed, and why each one has to be:
#   README.md              what each input is, how the corpus is rebuilt, what the
#                          gates actually check
#   templates.csv          hand-written patterns — our own work, unreproducible
#   authored_intents.csv   hand-written in-domain rows — likewise
#   assistant_test.csv     THE MODEL #4 EXAM. Never generate it, never train on it,
#                          and never edit a row because the model got it wrong.
#   assistant_test_meta.json / intents_meta.json
#                          sha256s, census and every gate receipt. These are what
#                          let a fresh clone VERIFY the corpus it rebuilds, and what
#                          make the exam's sha256 lock enforceable rather than a
#                          promise in a README.
!ml-service/data/assistant/README.md
!ml-service/data/assistant/templates.csv
!ml-service/data/assistant/authored_intents.csv
!ml-service/data/assistant/assistant_test.csv
!ml-service/data/assistant/assistant_test_meta.json
!ml-service/data/assistant/intents_meta.json

# scratch/patch scripts - never commit
.pt/
**/.doctmp_*
**/.m020*
